<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="notes-week-12" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Week 12</title>

<introduction>
    <p>
        This is an outline of the topics we covered in Week 12 of the course, from November 16 to 20.
    </p>
</introduction>

<handout component="cur">
    <title>Monday 11/16</title>
    <objectives>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Analyze functions to determine when they are increasing/decreasing and concave up/down.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Use the first and second derivative test to determine if a critical point is a local maximum or minimum.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Find inflection points of a graph.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </objectives>
    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Function Behaviors using the Derivative</title>

            <p>
                We saw last class that we can use the first derivative to determine where a function is increasing and decreasing.
            </p>

            <example workspace="4in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Determine the intervals of increase and decrease for function <m>f(x) = 2x^3 - 3x^2 - 12x + 5</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        
        </example>

        </paragraphs>
    </page>

    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>The First Derivative Test</title>
            <p component="b" workspace="3in">
                The first derivative test is a way to determine if a critical point is a local maximum or local minimum without plugging in values. How does our function have to be set up to get a local maximum?
            </p>
            <p component="a">
                The first derivative test is a way to determine if a critical point is a local maximum or local minimum without plugging in values. How does our function have to be set up to get a local maximum? Increase before, decrease after. This gives us the test.
            </p>
            
        </paragraphs>
    </page>

    <page>
        <example workspace="4in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Use the first derivative test to determine if the critical points of <m>f(x) = x^2 e^x</m> are local maxima or minima.
                </p>
            </statement>
        
        </example>
        
    </page>

    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>The Second Derivative</title>
            
            <p component="b" workspace="4in">
                If <m>f'(x)</m> measures the rate of change of the function <m>f(x)</m>, what does <m>f''(x)</m> measure?
            </p>
            <p component="a">
                If <m>f'(x)</m> measures the rate of change of the function <m>f(x)</m>, what does <m>f''(x)</m> measure? It's the rate of change of the slopes. If <m>f''(x) \gt 0</m>, then the slopes have to be increasing, which means we're above our tangent lines. This is what we mean by concave up.
            </p>

        </paragraphs>
    </page>

    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Test for Concavity</title>
            
            <p component="b" workspace="4in">
                How do we know if a function is concave up or concave down?
            </p>
            <p component="a">
                How do we know if a function is concave up or concave down? We use the second derivative.
            </p>
        </paragraphs>
    </page>

    <page>
        <p component="b" workspace="4in">
            So, given a function, how can we determine where it is concave up and concave down?
        </p>
        <p component="a">
            So, given a function, how can we determine where it is concave up and concave down? We need a sign chart for <m>f''(x)</m>.
        </p>
    </page>

    <page>
        <example workspace="0in">
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    Consider the function <md>
                        f(x) = \frac{x}{x^2 + 1}
                    </md>.
                    
                </p>
            </introduction>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Create a sign chart for <m>f</m>, <m>f'</m>, and <m>f''</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
        
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Determine on which intervals <m>f(x)</m> is increasing.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Determine on which intervals <m>f(x)</m> is decreasing.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Find and classify any local maxima or minima of <m>f(x)</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Determine on which intervals <m>f(x)</m> is concave up.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <task workspace="2in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Determine on which intervals <m>f(x)</m> is concave down.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        
        </example>
        
    </page>

    <page>
        <example workspace="0in">
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    Consider the function <md>
                        f(x) = \frac{x^2 - 8x}{x+1} \qquad f'(x) = \frac{x^2 + 2x - 8}{(x+1)^2} \qquad f''(x) = \frac{18}{(x+1)^3}
                    </md>.
                    
                </p>
            </introduction>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Create a sign chart for <m>f</m>, <m>f'</m>, and <m>f''</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
        
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Determine on which intervals <m>f(x)</m> is increasing.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Determine on which intervals <m>f(x)</m> is decreasing.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Find and classify any local maxima or minima of <m>f(x)</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Determine on which intervals <m>f(x)</m> is concave up.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <task workspace="2in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Determine on which intervals <m>f(x)</m> is concave down.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        
        </example>
        
    </page>

    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Inflection Points</title>
            
            <p component="b" workspace="4in">
                Inflection points somewhat play the role of critical points but for the second derivative. However, for these, we do want to guarantee a change in concavity.
            </p>
            <p component="a">
                Inflection points somewhat play the role of critical points but for the second derivative. However, for these, we do want to guarantee a change in concavity. What does this mean? Point in the domain of the function where the concavity changes sign.
            </p>
        </paragraphs>
    </page>

    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Second Derivative Test</title>
            
            <p component="b" workspace="4in">
                We can also use the second derivative to help us analyze critical points to see if they are local maxima or minima.
            </p>
            <p component="a">
                We can also use the second derivative to help us analyze critical points to see if they are local maxima or minima. If <m>f'' \gt 0</m>, the derivative is increasing, meaning it must go from negative to positive, hence local min. Also it looks like <m>x^2</m>.
            </p>
        </paragraphs>
    </page>

    <page>
        <example workspace="4in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find all critical points, inflection points, and use the second derivative test to analyze the critical points of <m>f(x) = 2x^3 - 3x^2 - 12x + 5</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        
        </example>
        
    </page>

    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Summary</title>
            <p component="b" workspace="4in">
                So, what have we learned and done so far?
            </p>
            <p component="a">
                So, what have we learned and done so far? 
            </p>
        </paragraphs>
    </page>
</handout>


<handout component="cur">
    <title>Tuesday 11/17</title>
    <objectives>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Define inflection point and learn how to use the second derivative test.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Sketch graphs of functions from derivative information.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </objectives>
    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Inflection Points</title>
            
            <p component="b" workspace="4in">
                Inflection points somewhat play the role of critical points but for the second derivative. However, for these, we do want to guarantee a change in concavity.
            </p>
            <p component="a">
                Inflection points somewhat play the role of critical points but for the second derivative. However, for these, we do want to guarantee a change in concavity. What does this mean? Point in the domain of the function where the concavity changes sign.
            </p>
        </paragraphs>
    </page>

    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Second Derivative Test</title>
            
            <p component="b" workspace="4in">
                We can also use the second derivative to help us analyze critical points to see if they are local maxima or minima.
            </p>
            <p component="a">
                We can also use the second derivative to help us analyze critical points to see if they are local maxima or minima. If <m>f'' \gt 0</m>, the derivative is increasing, meaning it must go from negative to positive, hence local min. Also it looks like <m>x^2</m>.
            </p>
        </paragraphs>
    </page>

    <page>
        <example workspace="4in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find all critical points, inflection points, and use the second derivative test to analyze the critical points of <m>f(x) = 2x^3 - 3x^2 - 12x + 5</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        
        </example>
        
    </page>

    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Graph Sketching</title>
            <p component="b" workspace="4in">
                So, what have we learned and done so far?
            </p>
            <p component="a">
                So, what have we learned and done so far? Increasing and decreasing, concavity, local max and min. Now we want to tie all of that together to help us sketch the graph of a function from derivative information. 
            </p>
        </paragraphs>
    </page>

    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Shape of Graphs</title>
            
        <p>
            The derivative tells us information about the graph of a function. What does that look like in terms of actual pictures?
